<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities not only in language generation but also in advancing scientific discovery. A growing body of work has explored ways to improve their reasoning, from self-consistency and chain-of-thought to multi-agent debate. Inspired by the dynamics of scientific committees and <em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">The Society of Mind</em>, we introduce <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span>, a complementary inference-time reasoning framework that performs inference through the weighted consensus of multiple LLMs. Our findings indicate that this approach significantly enhances reasoning in complex heterogeneous scientific tasks and improves scientific narratives in terms of creativity, rigor, and logical coherence, while reducing hallucinations that single models are prone to. Our approach emphasizes structured and interpretable consensus rather than opaque convergence, while requires only black-box access and uniform procedures, making it broadly applicable to multi-LLM reasoning. Project website at <a href="https://ai4scidisc.github.io/roundtable/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://ai4scidisc.github.io/roundtable/</a>.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated impressive capabilities in text generation, reasoning, and few-shot adaptation. These advances have opened exciting opportunities for scientific discovery, where complex, interdisciplinary problems demand not only accurate reasoning but also coherent narrative construction. However, current LLMs may hallucinate facts, introduce implausible logical jumps, or produce fragmented explanations. Such limitations are particularly problematic in scientific contexts, where reliability and clarity are equally critical. For instance, a scientific proposal must not only provide factually correct content, but also organize it into a structured and persuasive narrative. Addressing these dual requirements calls for mechanisms that go beyond the capacity of a single LLM.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A natural motivation is therefore to leverage multiple LLMs, aggregating their complementary strengths. Prior work has explored self-consistency with multiple sampled reasoning paths <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, multi-agent debate frameworks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du &amp; et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madaan &amp; et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and tool-augmented multi-agent systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schick et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghafarollahi &amp; Buehler, 2024</a>)</cite>. However, these approaches are often limited to single-task reasoning, rely on emergent dynamics that are difficult to interpret, or lack systematic mechanisms for weighting contributions across heterogeneous domains. Ensemble-style methods such as majority voting treat all models equally, overlooking that different models excel in different domains or evaluation dimensions. Debate-style methods, while sometimes converging to better outputs, remain inherently opaque: it is often unclear why a particular answer was selected, or hard to quantify which models were more trustworthy in the process. In scientific applications, this lack of transparency poses a major barrier, since researchers require not only correct outputs but also interpretable evidence supporting the decision.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.16839v1/new_pipeline_with_eg_fig.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:548/571;" width="548" height="571" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>
<span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview of the <span id="S1.F1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span>.</span>
An inference-time reasoning framework that produces consensus-weighted answers through fusion. Demonstrations for both structured (MultiTask) and open-ended (SingleTask) settings are included in Appendix §<a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Detailed Demonstration of Roundtable Policy ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">At a broader level, scientific problem solving is inherently multi-faceted, requiring both precise reasoning and coherent long-form narratives. While individual LLMs have made impressive progress, they still face challenges in consistently meeting these diverse demands across domains and tasks. This motivates the need for a structured approach to harness collective intelligence—one that integrates complementary expertise, surfaces and resolves disagreements transparently, and preserves accountability in the decision process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Building on these considerations, we introduce <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span>, an inference-time framework that formalizes deliberation among multiple LLMs into a principled process for trustworthy scientific reasoning and narrative generation (Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). In our approach, diverse models first propose candidate responses, which are then jointly evaluated to record their performances. These evaluations are distilled into a confidence-weight table that encodes both historical reliability and uncertainty for each candidate across tasks and dimensions. At inference, this confidence-weight table guides a fusion process that integrates the diverse responses into a single consensus answer—balancing factual accuracy with narrative coherence while preserving transparency in how the decision is reached. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, even on a scientific question such as explaining why nucleation accelerates at greater undercooling while growth dominates at smaller undercooling, individual models provide partial or inconsistent accounts. The roundtable process fuses these perspectives into a coherent explanation, showing how consensus-driven reasoning can elevate fragmented insights into a more complete scientific narrative.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span> in two complementary scientific regimes. In the <em id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MultiTask</em> setting, heterogeneous problems from geoscience, biology, mathematics, and materials science are solved simultaneously, testing cross-domain reasoning. In the <em id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">SingleTask</em> setting, models generate structured scientific proposals, testing long-form narrative construction. Across both, our findings show that <span id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span> consistently improves factual reliability, reasoning quality, and narrative coherence, yielding an average <span id="S1.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">13.01% improvement in MultiTask</span> and <span id="S1.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">11.04% improvement in SingleTask</span> compared to individual models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike ensemble-style methods such as majority voting, which treat all models equally, our approach adaptively weights models based on demonstrated reliability. And in contrast to debate-style frameworks, whose convergence dynamics are opaque, <span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span> produces explicit, auditable signals that make the aggregation process transparent. This combination of principled weighting and interpretability distinguishes our method as a scalable and accountable alternative for multi-model scientific reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, our contributions are threefold:
(i) we propose <span id="S1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span>, an inference-time framework for performance-weighted consensus aggregation among multiple LLMs;
(ii) we demonstrate that this approach enhances both factual reasoning and scientific narrative across heterogeneous tasks; and
(iii) we release a graduate-level materials science benchmark derived from UCLA PhD qualifying exams, which serves as a rigorous, domain-authentic testbed and complements existing evaluations of scientific LLMs. More broadly, the principles underlying <span id="S1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span> apply across multi-model scientific workflows, offering an interpretable and generalizable path toward AI-assisted reasoning and communication in science.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section formalizes the <span id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span> framework. We begin with the intuition behind the <span id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span> and then present the task settings and notation (a complete list of symbols is provided in Appendix §<a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Notation and Definitions ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>). We next describe how the confidence-weight table is learned from repeated interactions between players and graders, and how it subsequently guides inference through consensus-weighted fusion.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Design Intuitions behind <span id="S2.SS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Scientific decision-making rarely relies on a single voice. In research committees, for example, each member independently evaluates a proposal, but the final judgment is not determined by a simple vote. Instead, reviewers’ assessments are weighed by domain expertise, reliability, and the clarity of justification before being synthesized into a collective decision. This process balances agreement with accountability, ensuring that the outcome is both credible and transparent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span> draws on the same principle. Rather than treating LLMs as isolated predictors, we view them as members of a committee. A set of <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">players</em> propose candidate responses informed by diverse inductive biases. These are evaluated by an ensemble of <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">graders</em>, whose rubric-based assessments provide explicit signals of quality and uncertainty. Finally, a structured <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fusion step</em> aggregates these evaluations into a single consensus output—analogous to how a committee chair synthesizes diverse reviews into a reasoned decision.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This design is further motivated by the perspective of <em id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">The Society of Mind</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Minsky, 1988</a>)</cite>, which frames intelligence as the coordination of many specialized, relatively simple agents. By embracing this philosophy, <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span> transforms the inherent diversity of LLMs into a strength, enabling collective reasoning that is both interpretable and robust.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Task Settings</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span> in two complementary scientific regimes that mirror the dual demands of scientific reasoning: solving pointwise problems and constructing coherent narratives.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MultiTask.</span> Models are simultaneously queried with nine heterogeneous scientific questions spanning geoscience, biology, mathematics, and materials science (with six distinct sub-categories). Task formats include multiple-choice, fill-in-the-blank, and short-answer questions. Intuitively, this setting resembles a written exam with diverse problem types—success requires breadth of expertise rather than mastery of a single domain.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SingleTask.</span> Models are prompted to generate structured scientific proposals following a standardized three-paragraph template with sections for <em id="S2.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Background</em>, <em id="S2.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Methodology</em>, and <em id="S2.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Impact</em>. This setting captures the narrative aspect of scientific work: beyond producing isolated facts, one must weave them into a logically consistent and persuasive storyline.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Across both regimes, we deploy <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> pretrained LLMs (<em id="S2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">players</em>) to generate candidate responses and <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> LLMs (<em id="S2.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">graders</em>) to form the AI Committee. Just as in a real committee, players provide diverse answers while graders act as evaluators who judge each contribution against shared standards. In MultiTask, graders jointly evaluate the <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}\times N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}\times N</annotation></semantics></math> responses per round, analogous to examiners scoring a large pool of student answers. In SingleTask, graders assess proposals paragraph-by-paragraph along three rubric dimensions—creativity, scientific rigor, and logical coherence—reflecting the multifaceted criteria by which scientific proposals are judged.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Each evaluation yields a trust weight with two components: (i) a quality score in <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[-100,100]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>100,100</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[-100,100]</annotation></semantics></math>, where negative values indicate incorrect or misleading responses, and (ii) a calibrated 95% confidence interval representing grader certainty. Together, these form the equivalent of both a “grade” and a measure of “grading confidence,” ensuring that downstream decisions reflect not only evaluation outcomes but also their reliability. Notation follows Appendix §<a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Notation and Definitions ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{P}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>P</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{P}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th player agent, <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{G}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{G}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> the <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>-th grader, and <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>F</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{F}</annotation></semantics></math> the fusion agent. Player responses <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and grader scores <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s_{l}^{j},u_{l}^{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{l}^{j},u_{l}^{j})</annotation></semantics></math> are indexed by round <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Learning the Confidence-Weight Table</h3>

<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_figure ltx_float_algorithm ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle ltx_framed ltx_framed_top" style="width:397.5pt;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="alg1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> <span id="alg1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span>: Unified Training and Inference</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.6" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
              
              
            Dataset <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{p}</annotation></semantics></math> players, <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> graders, fusion agent <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>F</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, rounds <math id="alg1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
              
              
            Initialize confidence-weight table <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vartheta\leftarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϑ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vartheta\leftarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
              
              
              Sample query <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> (with key <math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> if MultiTask)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
              
              
              Players generate responses <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}^{j}\leftarrow A_{i}^{P}(q^{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>P</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}^{j}\leftarrow A_{i}^{P}(q^{j})</annotation></semantics></math>, collect <math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{j}=\{r_{i}^{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{j}=\{r_{i}^{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> Inference <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
              
              
               <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{j}\leftarrow A^{F}(r^{j},\vartheta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>F</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ϑ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{j}\leftarrow A^{F}(r^{j},\vartheta)</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l7.2" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="alg1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Consensus fusion
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">9:</span>
              
              
              Graders evaluate <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{j}</annotation></semantics></math>: <math id="alg1.l9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s_{l}^{j},u_{l}^{j})\leftarrow A_{l}^{G}(q^{j},r^{j},k^{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>G</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{l}^{j},u_{l}^{j})\leftarrow A_{l}^{G}(q^{j},r^{j},k^{j})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">10:</span>
              
              
              Aggregate <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s^{j},u^{j})\leftarrow\frac{1}{L_{g}}\sum_{l}(s_{l}^{j},u_{l}^{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mi>l</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s^{j},u^{j})\leftarrow\frac{1}{L_{g}}\sum_{l}(s_{l}^{j},u_{l}^{j})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">11:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">12:</span>
              
              
            Update <math id="alg1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vartheta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϑ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vartheta</annotation></semantics></math> using cumulative <math id="alg1.l12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s^{j},u^{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s^{j},u^{j})</annotation></semantics></math> for each player

</div>
<div id="alg1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">13:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vartheta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϑ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vartheta</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The confidence-weight table <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vartheta\in\mathbb{R}^{L_{p}\times N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϑ</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vartheta\in\mathbb{R}^{L_{p}\times N}</annotation></semantics></math> serves as a structured memory of each player’s historical reliability across subtasks (MultiTask) or rubric dimensions (SingleTask). Unlike fine-tuning approaches, <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span> does not alter the parameters of the underlying LLMs. Instead, <math id="S2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vartheta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϑ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vartheta</annotation></semantics></math> is iteratively updated from repeated rounds of evaluation, much like how a committee gradually learns which members are most reliable in which topics.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">At round <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>, a query <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled from the task dataset. Players generate responses <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}^{j}=A^{P}_{i}(q^{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>P</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}^{j}=A^{P}_{i}(q^{j})</annotation></semantics></math>, which are pooled into <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r^{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r^{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Graders then evaluate these responses, producing <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s_{l}^{j},u_{l}^{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>j</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{l}^{j},u_{l}^{j})</annotation></semantics></math>, which are averaged across <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> graders to yield <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s^{j},u^{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mi>j</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s^{j},u^{j})</annotation></semantics></math>. After <math id="S2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> rounds, the cumulative evaluations are distilled into:</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vartheta_{i,n}=\Biggl(\sum_{j=1}^{R}s^{j}_{i,n},\;\;\sum_{j=1}^{R}u^{j}_{i,n}\Biggr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϑ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>R</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.560em">,</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>R</mi></munderover><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>j</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo maxsize="2.600em" minsize="2.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vartheta_{i,n}=\Biggl(\sum_{j=1}^{R}s^{j}_{i,n},\;\;\sum_{j=1}^{R}u^{j}_{i,n}\Biggr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">capturing both the estimated quality and the associated uncertainty of each player. Intuitively, this table plays the role of a “track record” or “reputation system” for each player, ensuring that strong but inconsistent voices are weighted differently from consistently reliable ones. This design calibrates the committee’s trust in different players without retraining them, enabling adaptability and efficiency.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.4 </span>Inference with the Confidence-Weight Table</h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Once <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vartheta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϑ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vartheta</annotation></semantics></math> has been learned, it guides consensus fusion at inference. Given a new query <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, each player generates a response <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{i}=A^{P}_{i}(q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>P</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>q</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{i}=A^{P}_{i}(q)</annotation></semantics></math>. The collection <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\{r_{1},\dots,r_{L_{p}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\{r_{1},\dots,r_{L_{p}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is passed to the fusion process <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>F</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, which aggregates them using the pretrained <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vartheta^{\text{pretrained}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ϑ</mi><mtext>pretrained</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vartheta^{\text{pretrained}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}=A^{F}(r,\vartheta^{\text{pretrained}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>F</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>ϑ</mi><mtext>pretrained</mtext></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}=A^{F}(r,\vartheta^{\text{pretrained}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S2.SS4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vartheta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϑ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vartheta</annotation></semantics></math> ensures that expertise is applied where it matters most. In MultiTask, it emphasizes domain-specific reliability (e.g., assigning higher weights to players consistently strong in mathematics). In SingleTask, it captures stylistic and analytical strengths across rubric dimensions, reflecting how different voices contribute to different aspects of a proposal.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The fusion process itself is not fine-tuned; it acts as an orchestrator that conditions on <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vartheta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϑ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vartheta</annotation></semantics></math> to produce a consensus answer. This mimics how a committee chair integrates both the content and credibility of reviewers before issuing a final recommendation. Optionally, the AI Committee can re-evaluate <math id="S2.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{r}</annotation></semantics></math> to assign a quality score and confidence interval, preserving interpretability and accountability throughout the process.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design experiments to systematically evaluate the effectiveness and robustness of the proposed <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span>. Our analysis is guided by the following research questions:</p>
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1:</span> To what extent does <span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span> enhance scientific reasoning and writing performance relative to individual LLMs? (see §<a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Performance Improvement (RQ1) ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2:</span> How stable and robust is <span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span> over long-run evaluation regimes, and does it consistently provide more reliable performance? (see §<a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Long-Run Stability and Robustness (RQ2) ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3:</span> To what degree do AI graders introduce bias, and can the AI Committee mitigate these biases to achieve greater inter-grader consistency? (see §<a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Bias and Consistency of AI Graders (RQ3) ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>)</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Performance Improvement (RQ1)</h3>

<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span><span id="S3.T1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmark performance of individual LLMs versus <span id="S3.T1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span> (RP).</span> Top: accuracy on nine heterogeneous scientific tasks in the <em id="S3.T1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MultiTask</em>. Bottom: rubric-based scores on scientific proposal writing in the <em id="S3.T1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">SingleTask</em>. Red marks the overall best result while blue the second best.</figcaption>
<div id="S3.T1.8" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:397.5pt;height:153.4pt;vertical-align:-75.4pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-179.6pt,69.3pt) scale(0.525311789216877,0.525311789216877) ;">
<table id="S3.T1.8.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S3.T1.8.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="position:relative; bottom:-6.5pt;">Setting</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.8.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="position:relative; bottom:-6.5pt;">Model</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.8.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;" colspan="6"><span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Material Science Dataset</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.8.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;" colspan="3"><span id="S3.T1.8.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Others</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.8.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.8.1.2.1" class="ltx_td" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"></td>
<td id="S3.T1.8.1.2.2" class="ltx_td" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"></td>
<td id="S3.T1.8.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S3.T1.8.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mech. 1<math id="S3.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.8.1.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S3.T1.8.1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Mech. 2<math id="S3.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.8.1.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S3.T1.8.1.2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">EE<math id="S3.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.8.1.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S3.T1.8.1.2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Opt.<math id="S3.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.8.1.2.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S3.T1.8.1.2.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Thermo.<math id="S3.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.8.1.2.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S3.T1.8.1.2.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Semi.<math id="S3.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.8.1.2.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S3.T1.8.1.2.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Math<math id="S3.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.8.1.2.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S3.T1.8.1.2.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Geo.<math id="S3.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.8.1.2.11" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;"><span id="S3.T1.8.1.2.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bio.<math id="S3.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.8.1.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.8.1.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;" rowspan="10"><span id="S3.T1.8.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:6.9pt;height:48.1pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="width:48.1pt;transform:translate(-20.6pt,-20.6pt) rotate(-90deg) ;">
<span id="S3.T1.8.1.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">MultiTask</span>
</span></span></span></td>
