</span></div>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To answer <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ1</span>, we compare <span id="S3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span> (RT) against individual LLM baselines across both MultiTask and SingleTask settings. Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.1 Performance Improvement (RQ1) ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes overall performance, while Figures <a href="#S3.F2.sf1" title="In Figure 2 ‣ Quantitative Analysis. ‣ 3.1 Performance Improvement (RQ1) ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(a)</span></a>–<a href="#S3.F2.sf2" title="In Figure 2 ‣ Quantitative Analysis. ‣ 3.1 Performance Improvement (RQ1) ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(b)</span></a> visualize relative gains. This correspondence between player ID and model follows Table <a href="#A1.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ A.1 Symbols and Abbreviations ‣ Appendix A Notation and Definitions ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. The correspondence of subtasks of MultiTask and task IDs follows Table <a href="#A3.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Materials-science subset. ‣ C.1 Dataset Annotation ‣ Appendix C Benchmarking Datasets ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Quantitative Analysis.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the MultiTask regime, RT delivers a 13.01% average lift, outperforming 8 out of 9 single-model baselines. The only exception is Task 9 (Bio.), where Qwen-plus (<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="51.64\pm 6.57" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>51.64</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>6.57</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">51.64\pm 6.57</annotation></semantics></math>) and GPT-4o (<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="51.53\pm 6.64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>51.53</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>6.64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">51.53\pm 6.64</annotation></semantics></math>) slightly exceed RT (<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="47.55\pm 7.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>47.55</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>7.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">47.55\pm 7.02</annotation></semantics></math>). Beyond accuracy, RT reduces uncertainty; for instance, on Task 7 (Math) the confidence interval contracts to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 1.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 1.01</annotation></semantics></math> compared with baselines’ <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 1.70" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>1.70</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 1.70</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.68" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2.68</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.68</annotation></semantics></math>. Figure <a href="#S3.F2.sf1" title="In Figure 2 ‣ Quantitative Analysis. ‣ 3.1 Performance Improvement (RQ1) ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(a)</span></a> further shows per-player improvements. Gains are broadly positive on Tasks 1–8, with especially large margins on reasoning-intensive subtasks—for example, +90.5% over Gemini-2.0 on Task 7. Smaller improvements occur when a single model already aligns closely with ground truth, while Task 9 shows marginal or negative differences—consistent with its per-task averages. Appendix §<a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1 MultiTask ‣ Appendix D Fine-grained Results ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a> confirms that weaker players (e.g., Gemini-2.0) enjoy the largest lifts, while stronger models exhibit limited headroom.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the SingleTask regime, which evaluates structured scientific proposal writing, RT achieves the best performance in all nine rubric cells, explaining the observed 11.04% average lift. Each proposal comprises three sections—Background, Methodology, and Impact—scored across Creativity, Scientific Rigor, and Logical Coherence. Scores cluster tightly around 90–91% with narrow confidence intervals (typically <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm 3.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><mn>3.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm 3.1</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.9</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.9</annotation></semantics></math>). Gains concentrate in the Impact section, particularly Scientific Rigor, as shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F2.sf2" title="In Figure 2 ‣ Quantitative Analysis. ‣ 3.1 Performance Improvement (RQ1) ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2(b)</span></a>. Appendix §<a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 SingleTask ‣ Appendix D Fine-grained Results ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a> reveals positive medians across most players, with right-skewed tails indicating that even when consensus does not outperform the strongest model, it still elevates weaker drafts significantly.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.16839v1/multitask_2d_gain.svg" id="S3.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:548/469;" width="548" height="469"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(a) </span>Relative gains(%) in the <em id="S3.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MultiTask</em>.</figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S3.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.16839v1/singletask_2d_gain.svg" id="S3.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:548/504;" width="548" height="504"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(b) </span>Relative gains(%) in the <em id="S3.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">SingleTask</em>.</figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S3.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Performance improvement by <span id="S3.F2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Qualitative Analysis.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These results illustrate how RT acts like a committee process rather than a flat average. In MultiTask, the confidence-weight table down-weights unreliable contributions and amplifies complementary strengths, thereby “rescuing” weaker players without diluting strong ones. This mirrors how, in a committee, flawed proposals can still contribute useful fragments when evaluated critically. The large gains on reasoning-heavy tasks (e.g., Tasks 6–7) highlight how diverse error patterns across models can be combined into substantially stronger reasoning chains.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In SingleTask writing, weaker players show the most pronounced gains in Impact and Methodology. For instance, GPT-4o exhibits long right tails in Impact–Scientific Rigor and Impact–Logical Coherence, while GPT-o3 shows consistently positive medians with bounded variance. Even strong baselines such as DS-chat rarely degrade under RT, suggesting that consensus seldom harms high-quality drafts but frequently elevates weaker ones. Mechanistically, the confidence-weight table enforces rubric-aware, non-redundant fusion: systematically weak contributors are down-weighted, while demonstrated strengths are amplified. The pattern resembles how peer reviewers specialize—some focusing on methodology, others on impact—and the editor synthesizes these perspectives into a well-rounded final decision.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Long-Run Stability and Robustness (RQ2)</h3>

<figure id="S3.T2" class="ltx_table">
