</span></span></span></p>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To answer <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ2</span>, we test whether RT’s gains remain statistically reliable over long-run evaluations. Using the Wilcoxon signed-rank test (§<a href="#A1.SS2" title="A.2 Formal Hypothesis Testing Framework for Wilcoxon Signed-Rank Significance Validity ‣ Appendix A Notation and Definitions ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.2</span></a>) on round-level paired differences, RT consistently outperforms all baselines. As shown in Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.2 Long-Run Stability and Robustness (RQ2) ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, across all 18 models RT achieves significant improvements at <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-values typically below <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-40}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-40}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Quantitative Analysis.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the MultiTask regime, RT consistently outperforms baselines with mean percentage gains ranging from +5.3% (Grok-2) to +46.5% (Gemini-2.0). Even strong players such as GPT-4o (+6.5%) and Claude-3.7Sonnet (+13.7%) see reliable gains, highlighting that consensus remains beneficial even when individual agents are already competitive. The large improvement over Gemini-2.0 illustrates how RT effectively leverages complementary strengths to elevate weaker contributors, rather than letting them dilute overall performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the SingleTask regime, RT delivers significant lifts across all nine baselines, with mean improvements from +7.2% (Deepseek-Chat) to +27.2% (GPT-4o-mini). Notably, gains are especially pronounced for smaller or lighter-weight models (e.g., GPT-4o-mini, GPT-4o), suggesting that RT acts as a stabilizing mechanism that brings their drafts closer to the quality of stronger models. Even against advanced systems like GPT-o3 and Claude-3.7Sonnet, improvements remain consistently positive, confirming robustness across a wide range of capability levels.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Qualitative Analysis.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond statistical significance, the long-run results highlight important qualitative patterns. In the MultiTask regime, RT’s robustness stems from its ability to repeatedly exploit complementary error profiles: weaker players such as Gemini-2.0 or Llama-3.3 often fail in different ways, but when their contributions are weighted through the confidence table, the ensemble consistently extracts correct elements while suppressing noise. This is reflected in the large variance reported in Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.2 Long-Run Stability and Robustness (RQ2) ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>—although individual rounds fluctuate, the aggregated effect over thousands of rounds converges to stable gains. In committee terms, even if certain members are unreliable in a given discussion, their occasional correct insights are preserved while systematic mistakes are filtered out, ensuring dependable group performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In the SingleTask regime, stability is most visible at the rubric level. Analytical dimensions such as Impact–Scientific Rigor show the largest sustained gains, whereas stylistic traits (e.g., Creativity or Logical Coherence) improve more modestly. This asymmetry suggests that RT is especially effective in consolidating rigorous, reasoning-heavy contributions—akin to how editors place greater weight on technical soundness than on stylistic flair when synthesizing reviewer reports. Importantly, the consistency of improvements across tens of thousands of rounds demonstrates that RT does not rely on one-off synergies, but instead behaves like a well-tuned review board: repeatedly integrating diverse perspectives into a balanced and accountable consensus.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Bias and Consistency of AI Graders (RQ3)</h3>

<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.16839v1/bias_fig.png" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/206;" width="548" height="206" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>
<span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bias and consistency of AI graders.</span>
The outer line plots show the rank distributions assigned by four independent arbitrators, while the inset heatmaps display their pairwise inter-grader agreement measured by Kendall’s Tau.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To address <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">RQ3</span>, we examine grader bias and inter-grader consistency through two complementary lenses: (i) the distribution of ranks assigned to RT by different graders, and (ii) pairwise agreement measured by Kendall’s Tau (see §<a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Formal Framework for Kendall’s Tau Rank Correlation Analysis ‣ Appendix A Notation and Definitions ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a>). Aggregated results are shown in Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.3 Bias and Consistency of AI Graders (RQ3) ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, with task- and dimension-level breakdowns provided in Figures <a href="#A4.F14" title="Figure 14 ‣ Implications. ‣ D.3 AI Graders ‣ Appendix D Fine-grained Results ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> and <a href="#A4.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ Implications. ‣ D.3 AI Graders ‣ Appendix D Fine-grained Results ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> in §<a href="#A4.SS3" title="D.3 AI Graders ‣ Appendix D Fine-grained Results ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Quantitative Analysis.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S3.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 3.3 Bias and Consistency of AI Graders (RQ3) ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> highlights a clear distinction between MultiTask and SingleTask regimes.
In MultiTask, the aggregated rank distribution is steep and concentrated in the top three positions, with RT frequently ranked first. Correspondingly, Kendall’s Tau values are consistently high (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.64" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.64</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.64</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.72" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.72</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.72</annotation></semantics></math>), reflecting strong alignment among graders. Task-level inspection (Figures <a href="#A4.F14" title="Figure 14 ‣ Implications. ‣ D.3 AI Graders ‣ Appendix D Fine-grained Results ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, <a href="#A4.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ Implications. ‣ D.3 AI Graders ‣ Appendix D Fine-grained Results ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) confirms this pattern: Tasks 001–006 exhibit overlapping distributions, Tasks 007–008 achieve near-perfect consensus (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau&gt;0.85" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.85</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau&gt;0.85</annotation></semantics></math>), while Task 009 shows the weakest agreement (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\approx 0.49" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.49</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\approx 0.49</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.59" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.59</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.59</annotation></semantics></math>) as some graders disperse RT’s rank across mid-range positions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In SingleTask, however, the aggregated rank distribution is flatter and more dispersed, and Kendall’s Tau values drop markedly (<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.15</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.25" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.25</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.25</annotation></semantics></math> on average). Creativity-related dimensions (e.g., Background–Creativity, Methodology–Creativity) place RT near the top but with substantial grader variability. Logical coherence dimensions show the lowest alignment, with Tau values as low as <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\approx 0.07" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.07</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\approx 0.07</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.14</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.14</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating inconsistent judgments. By contrast, Impact–Scientific Rigor yields the strongest agreement, with RT concentrated near rank 1 and Tau values of <math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\approx 0.23" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>0.23</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\approx 0.23</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.34" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.34</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.34</annotation></semantics></math>. Together, these results demonstrate that graders achieve high consensus in objective, structured tasks but diverge significantly when assessing subjective, open-ended proposals.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Qualitative Analysis.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These results reveal that bias is minimal in structured MultiTask settings, where evaluation criteria are well defined, but more pronounced in open-ended SingleTask writing, where subjective interpretation shapes grading. This mirrors human peer review: reviewers evaluating technical correctness tend to agree, while judgments about creativity or style diverge. Crucially, the AI Committee mitigates such variability by aggregating across graders, reducing the impact of individual biases. In practice, this means RT functions like an editorial board: even when reviewers disagree, their collective judgment yields a more balanced and reliable outcome.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Ablation Study</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To disentangle the contributions of our design choices and validate their scientific relevance, we conduct two ablation studies. These analyses illustrate how <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span> strengthens structured reasoning and improves narrative quality by highlighting what is gained—and what is lost—when key components are removed.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.16839v1/policy_ablation_fig.svg" id="S4.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/206;" width="548" height="206"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span> <span id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Policy Ablation.</span> Comparison of Roundtable Policy (RT) with Best-Single-Model Policy.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Policy Ablation.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first compare RT with a Best-Single-Model Policy (BSMP), which simply selects the response from the single model with the highest confidence weight rather than synthesizing across multiple players. Conceptually, BSMP resembles a committee that crowns one “top performer” and ignores all other voices. The results in Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 4 Ablation Study ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> make clear why this approach falls short: across both MultiTask and SingleTask settings, RT consistently outperforms BSMP. In MultiTask, the advantages are particularly evident in reasoning-heavy domains such as mathematics and mechanics, where drawing on complementary perspectives yields large gains. In SingleTask proposal writing, consensus fusion not only raises average scores across all rubric dimensions but also reduces variance, producing outputs that are both stronger and more consistent. These findings highlight that scientific progress rarely emerges from a single dominant voice; it is the synthesis of multiple viewpoints that produces reliable reasoning and coherent narrative.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.16839v1/rounds_ablation_fig.svg" id="S4.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/184;" width="548" height="184"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span> <span id="S4.F5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Rounds Ablation.</span> Effect of the rounds used to build the confidence-weight table.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Rounds Ablation.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next examine the impact of the number of training rounds used to construct the confidence-weight table. Here, the table plays the role of institutional memory—accumulating evidence about which players are dependable under different conditions. As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ Policy Ablation. ‣ 4 Ablation Study ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, the effect of this memory differs by task type. In MultiTask, where benchmarks span diverse scientific domains, performance steadily improves as more rounds are incorporated and stabilizes only after hundreds of evaluations. By contrast, SingleTask writing converges much more quickly: fewer than 100 rounds already suffice to surpass baselines, with further rounds offering only modest additional benefit. This divergence reflects a natural distinction: heterogeneous domains require extensive calibration to build trust across disciplines, whereas rubric-based writing tasks benefit from faster stabilization since evaluative criteria are more homogeneous.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Summary.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These ablations underscore two core insights: First, weighted consensus is fundamentally more powerful than relying on a single “best” model, as robust science arises from collective deliberation rather than solitary voices. Second, the effectiveness of the confidence-weight table depends on task structure: structured MultiTask settings demand long-run calibration, while open-ended SingleTask writing achieves stability with much less historical data. Together, these findings reinforce that consensus-driven fusion is not only effective but also principled—mirroring how real scientific communities build reliable knowledge through collective judgment.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LLMs for Scientific Discovery.</span>
Large language models (LLMs) have rapidly expanded the scope of data-driven science, from AlphaFold’s breakthroughs in structural biology <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jumper et al., 2021</a>)</cite> to recent applications across mathematics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yue et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, chemistry <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, biology <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Labrak et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and geography <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Their strength lies in structured reasoning and seamless integration with external tools, enabling automated workflows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao, 2017</a>)</cite>. Yet, complex multi-step reasoning often requires specialized prompting, tool-augmented inference, or fine-tuning, motivating the rise of agentic systems that combine reasoning with external solvers and databases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Boiko et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reasoning and Factuality in Language Models.</span>
Improving reasoning and factuality in LLMs has been studied extensively. Reasoning can be enhanced through prompting methods such as scratchpads <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nye &amp; et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, chain-of-thought demonstrations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kojima et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reynolds &amp; McDonell, 2021</a>)</cite>, verification and self-consistency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, and reflection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shinn et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madaan &amp; et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, as well as fine-tuning approaches <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rajani et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zelikman et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewkowycz et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Factuality, in turn, has been strengthened through RLHF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ziegler &amp; et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, dataset curation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee &amp; et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, retrieval-based augmentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guu et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, and likelihood-based calibration <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kadavath &amp; et al., 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Multi-Agent and Ensemble Approaches.</span>
Beyond single-model prompting, researchers have explored ensemble and multi-agent strategies to enhance reliability. Early work shows that self-consistency <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022</a>)</cite> and majority voting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cobbe et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thoppilan et al., 2022</a>)</cite> can mitigate errors by aggregating diverse reasoning chains. Building on this, multi-agent debate frameworks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Irving et al., 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du &amp; et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Madaan &amp; et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and collaborative dialogue methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chan &amp; et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang &amp; et al., 2023</a>)</cite> let models iteratively critique and refine one another’s outputs. Related lines of work also investigate compositional generation, combining outputs from multiple models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu &amp; et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alayrac &amp; et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeng &amp; et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. While these methods demonstrate the value of collective intelligence, they often rely on flat aggregation or emergent convergence. In contrast, our approach introduces a structured consensus mechanism that explicitly weights models by demonstrated reliability, producing interpretable and auditable signals of trust.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Benchmarks for LLM-Based Agents.</span>
Evaluating agentic LLMs has inspired benchmarks ranging from synthetic tool-use tasks (e.g., TaskBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>) to domain-specific challenges such as SWE-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jimenez et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, BioCoder-Py <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and MLAgentBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. More recently, datasets sourced from scientific literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Majumder et al., 2024</a>)</cite> aim to capture authentic workflows. Our work complements these efforts by introducing a domain-authentic benchmark in materials science that stresses both mechanistic understanding and property prediction, thereby testing agents under both interpretative and predictive dimensions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Machine Learning and Agents for Materials Science.</span>
Machine learning has already transformed materials science by enabling scalable property prediction, compound discovery, and structure–property analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jain et al., 2013</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Butler et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, with advances in reinforcement learning for inverse design <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Segler et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, generative modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Granda et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, and multi-modal integration of simulations and experiments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pyzer-Knapp et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. These developments reduce experimental costs and provide interpretable insights, but LLM-based agents remain relatively underexplored in this domain. The inherent complexity of materials science—multiscale interactions, vast compositional spaces, and intricate synthesis pipelines—calls for agent frameworks that unify predictive modeling with experimental feedback. Early systems in autonomous discovery <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gregoire et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Maurizi et al., 2024</a>)</cite> show promise, but important gaps remain in linking reasoning, planning, and uncertainty quantification. Our work positions materials science as a natural testbed for stress-testing multi-agent reasoning frameworks such as <span id="S5.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span>, bridging structured evaluation and open-ended scientific narrative.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Conclusion &amp; Future Directions</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We presented the <span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span>, a inference-time reasoning framework that fuses outputs from multiple LLMs through confidence-weighted aggregation without finetuning base models. Unlike single-model prompting or naive ensemble methods such as majority voting, our approach does not treat all models equally nor rely on opaque emergent convergence, as in debate-style methods. Instead, it introduces a principled mechanism that explicitly encodes historical reliability and uncertainty, producing consensus answers that are not only more accurate but also transparent and auditable. Across structured <em id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MultiTask</em> benchmarks and open-ended <em id="S6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">SingleTask</em> proposal writing, we showed that <span id="S6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span> consistently improves factual reliability, reasoning consistency, and narrative coherence, highlighting its value as a trustworthy tool for scientific reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Looking forward, several avenues can extend this framework. First, <em id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">adaptive arbitrator strategies</em> could allow the confidence-weight table to evolve dynamically with task complexity, reallocating trust in real time. Second, coupling the fusion process with <em id="S6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reinforcement learning</em> and human or experimental feedback may enable autonomous optimization of weight assignments through trial and error. Third, incorporating richer feedback signals—such as calibrated uncertainty, tool-augmented checks, or domain-specific rubrics—could further strengthen robustness in complex scientific workflows.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">More broadly, the principles underlying <span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span> generalize beyond the settings studied here. By tailoring consensus mechanisms to domain-specific evaluation criteria, the framework can support applications ranging from drug discovery and computational chemistry to astrophysics and other data-intensive sciences. In this sense, <span id="S6.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span> is not merely another ensemble method, but a scalable and interpretable paradigm for collective intelligence in LLMs—one that advances the rigor, reliability, and accountability of AI-assisted scientific discovery.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Ethics Statement</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our method significantly enhances the performance of sequential model editing, making it invaluable for updating and managing knowledge in real-world applications. While the ability to directly modify stored knowledge introduces potential risks, such as the introduction of false or harmful information, we strongly urge researchers to implement strict validation and oversight to ensure the ethical use of these techniques. Nevertheless, the original goal of model editing is positive, aiming to facilitate efficient updates of large models in the future. Therefore, we encourage researchers to leverage this technology responsibly and with care.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Reproducibility</h2>

<div id="Sx2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure the reproducibility of our findings, detailed implementation instructions for <span id="Sx2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span> can be found in Appendix. Additionally, the source code is publicly available at <a href="https://ai4scidisc.github.io/roundtable/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://ai4scidisc.github.io/roundtable/</a>. These measures are intended to facilitate the verification and replication of our results by other researchers in the field.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledement</h2>

<div id="Sx3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We gratefully acknowledge the <a href="https://supercloud.mit.edu/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">MIT SuperCloud</a> and <a href="http://www.tacc.utexas.edu" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_href">Texas Advanced Computing Center (TACC)</a> at The University of Texas at Austin for providing computational resources that have contributed to the research results reported within this paper. JL acknowledges support by the Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency (DARPA) under Agreement No. HR00112490369. The content is solely the responsibility of the authors and does not necessarily represent the official views of the funding entities.</p>
</div>
</section>
