<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Formal Hypothesis Testing Framework for Wilcoxon Signed-Rank Significance Validity</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To rigorously assess whether the Roundtable Policy (RT) provides statistically significant improvements over individual LLM baselines, we apply the non-parametric <span id="A1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Wilcoxon signed-rank test</span> to paired, round-level performance data. This test evaluates whether the median of the differences between two matched samples significantly exceeds zero, under the null hypothesis of no improvement.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Notation.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the set of evaluation rounds and <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}=\{p_{1},\dots,p_{L}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}=\{p_{1},\dots,p_{L}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> baseline players.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We define:</p>
<ul id="A1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{r}^{(\texttt{RT})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">RT</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{r}^{(\texttt{RT})}</annotation></semantics></math>: score obtained by RT in round <math id="A1.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\in\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\in\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{r}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{r}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math>: score obtained by player <math id="A1.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> in the same round,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{r}:=\dfrac{s_{r}^{(\texttt{RT})}-s_{r}^{(p)}}{|s_{r}^{(p)}|}\times 100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">RT</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{r}:=\dfrac{s_{r}^{(\texttt{RT})}-s_{r}^{(p)}}{|s_{r}^{(p)}|}\times 100</annotation></semantics></math>: relative gain of RT over player <math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> in round <math id="A1.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> (as percentage).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Hypothesis Test.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct the one-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank test:</p>
<table id="A1.E3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A1.E3X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E3X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E3X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:\text{Median}(g_{r})\leq 0\quad\text{(no improvement)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mtext>Median</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>(no improvement)</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:\text{Median}(g_{r})\leq 0\quad\text{(no improvement)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A1.E3Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E3Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle H_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle H_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E3Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle:\text{Median}(g_{r})&gt;0\quad\text{(RT improves over }p\text{)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mtext>Median</mtext><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>(RT improves over </mtext><mi>p</mi><mtext>)</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle:\text{Median}(g_{r})&gt;0\quad\text{(RT improves over }p\text{)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Test Procedure.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given a sample of paired differences <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{g_{r}\}_{r\in\mathcal{R}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{g_{r}\}_{r\in\mathcal{R}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we perform:</p>
<ol id="A1.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="A1.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Discard entries with <math id="A1.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{r}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{r}=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Rank the absolute values: <math id="A1.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{rank}(|g_{r}|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>rank</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{rank}(|g_{r}|)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Compute the signed-rank statistic:</p>
<table id="A1.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="W^{+}=\sum_{r:g_{r}&gt;0}\text{rank}(|g_{r}|)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow></munder><mtext>rank</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{+}=\sum_{r:g_{r}&gt;0}\text{rank}(|g_{r}|).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="A1.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using the null distribution of <math id="A1.I3.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> (or a normal approximation), we calculate a <math id="A1.I3.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-value.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Statistical significance is established if:</p>
<table id="A1.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;\alpha=0.05." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;\alpha=0.05.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Continuity Correction for Normal Approximation.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under the null hypothesis <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, the test statistic <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W^{+}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W^{+}</annotation></semantics></math> approximately follows a normal distribution when the sample size <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is large (typically <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 20</annotation></semantics></math>). In this case, we compute a <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-score using the continuity-corrected normal approximation:</p>
<table id="A1.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=\frac{W^{+}-\frac{n(n+1)}{4}-0.5}{\sqrt{\frac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{24}}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>W</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>24</mn></mfrac></msqrt></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=\frac{W^{+}-\frac{n(n+1)}{4}-0.5}{\sqrt{\frac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}{24}}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of non-zero <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> values (i.e., rounds with non-zero paired differences), and <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math> is the continuity correction term to account for the discreteness of the rank sum distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The resulting <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math>-score is then used to compute a one-sided <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-value based on the standard normal distribution. This approximation is used in our evaluation whenever <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> exceeds the small-sample regime (<math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 20</annotation></semantics></math>), ensuring computational efficiency without sacrificing statistical validity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.2.1 </span>Application to <span id="A1.SS2.SSS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MultiTask</span> Evaluation</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the MultiTask evaluation, each task represents a distinct skill domain (e.g., math, geoscience, mechanics). Let <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}=\{t_{1},\dots,t_{K}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}=\{t_{1},\dots,t_{K}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=9</annotation></semantics></math> benchmark tasks.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each player <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and task <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{k}\in\mathcal{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{k}\in\mathcal{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and for each round <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute the percentage gain:</p>
<table id="A1.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{i}^{(k)}=\frac{s_{i,t_{k}}^{(\texttt{RT})}-s_{i,t_{k}}^{(p)}}{|s_{i,t_{k}}^{(p)}|}\times 100." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">RT</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{i}^{(k)}=\frac{s_{i,t_{k}}^{(\texttt{RT})}-s_{i,t_{k}}^{(p)}}{|s_{i,t_{k}}^{(p)}|}\times 100.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We test each pair <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(p,t_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(p,t_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> independently. To assess holistic advantage, we also aggregate over tasks:</p>
<table id="A1.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{\text{agg}}^{(p)}=\bigcup_{k=1}^{K}\left\{g_{i}^{(k)}\right\}_{i=1}^{N_{k}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐠</mi><mtext>agg</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">⋃</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{\text{agg}}^{(p)}=\bigcup_{k=1}^{K}\left\{g_{i}^{(k)}\right\}_{i=1}^{N_{k}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We consider RT statistically superior to player <math id="A1.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> if:</p>
<ul id="A1.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the Wilcoxon test yields <math id="A1.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math> on at least 8 of 9 tasks, or</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the aggregate test on <math id="A1.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{\text{agg}}^{(p)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐠</mi><mtext>agg</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{\text{agg}}^{(p)}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="A1.I4.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.05</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">A.2.2 </span>Application to <span id="A1.SS2.SSS2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">SingleTask</span> Evaluation</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the SingleTask evaluation, each round produces rubric-based scores over 9 dimensions <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{d_{1},\dots,d_{9}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>9</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{d_{1},\dots,d_{9}\}</annotation></semantics></math> (covering three traits for each of Background, Methodology, and Impact).</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For each player <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>, round <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math>, and dimension <math id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\in\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\in\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute:</p>
<table id="A1.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{r}^{(d)}=\frac{s_{r}^{(\texttt{RT}),d}-s_{r}^{(p),d}}{|s_{r}^{(p),d}|}\times 100." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">RT</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{r}^{(d)}=\frac{s_{r}^{(\texttt{RT}),d}-s_{r}^{(p),d}}{|s_{r}^{(p),d}|}\times 100.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct Wilcoxon tests:</p>
<ul id="A1.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Per-dimension</span>: for each <math id="A1.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> independently, to assess fine-grained quality improvements.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Aggregate-level</span>: pool all scores:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{g}_{\text{agg}}^{(p)}=\{g_{r}^{(d)}:r\in\mathcal{R},d\in\mathcal{D}\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐠</mi><mtext>agg</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{g}_{\text{agg}}^{(p)}=\{g_{r}^{(d)}:r\in\mathcal{R},d\in\mathcal{D}\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.I5.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">and test for overall statistical significance.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Interpretation.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS2.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The MultiTask analysis evaluates whether RT consistently improves performance across structurally diverse problems and domains. The SingleTask analysis inspects RT’s advantage across nuanced dimensions of academic writing quality. In both cases, aggregate-level tests offer a robust indicator of RT’s overall statistical superiority over individual LLM baselines.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.3 </span>Formal Framework for Kendall’s Tau Rank Correlation Analysis</h3>

<div id="A1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To quantify the agreement between different graders’ rankings, we adopt the Kendall’s Tau rank correlation coefficient <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kendall, 1938</a>)</cite>, a non-parametric statistic that measures the ordinal association between two rankings.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Mathematical Definition.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{(a)}=(\pi^{(a)}_{1},\dots,\pi^{(a)}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(a)}=(\pi^{(a)}_{1},\dots,\pi^{(a)}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{(b)}=(\pi^{(b)}_{1},\dots,\pi^{(b)}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{(b)}=(\pi^{(b)}_{1},\dots,\pi^{(b)}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the rankings of <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> items (players) produced by graders <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. For each unordered pair <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&lt;j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&lt;j</annotation></semantics></math>, define:</p>
<table id="A1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sign}_{a}(i,j)=\operatorname{sign}(\pi^{(a)}_{i}-\pi^{(a)}_{j}),\quad\text{sign}_{b}(i,j)=\operatorname{sign}(\pi^{(b)}_{i}-\pi^{(b)}_{j})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>sign</mtext><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>sign</mtext><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>sign</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sign}_{a}(i,j)=\operatorname{sign}(\pi^{(a)}_{i}-\pi^{(a)}_{j}),\quad\text{sign}_{b}(i,j)=\operatorname{sign}(\pi^{(b)}_{i}-\pi^{(b)}_{j}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">A pair <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> is called <em id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">concordant</em> if <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sign}_{a}(i,j)=\text{sign}_{b}(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>sign</mtext><mi>a</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>sign</mtext><mi>b</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sign}_{a}(i,j)=\text{sign}_{b}(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math> and <em id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.2.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">discordant</em> otherwise. Let <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> be the numbers of concordant and discordant pairs, respectively. Then Kendall’s Tau is defined as</p>
<table id="A1.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(a,b)\;=\;\frac{C-D}{\tbinom{n}{2}}\;\in[-1,1]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac linethickness="0pt"><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.558em">∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(a,b)\;=\;\frac{C-D}{\tbinom{n}{2}}\;\in[-1,1].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Here <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=1</annotation></semantics></math> indicates perfect agreement, <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=-1</annotation></semantics></math> perfect disagreement, and <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0</annotation></semantics></math> statistical independence of the rankings.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Application to MultiTask.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the MultiTask setting, each task <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in\{1,\dots,9\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in\{1,\dots,9\}</annotation></semantics></math> yields a separate ranking of player models by each grader. For every round <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> and task <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{r,t}(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{r,t}(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> for all pairs of graders <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> using their per-round rankings of the nine players. Averaging over all rounds gives</p>
<table id="A1.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\tau}_{t}(a,b)=\frac{1}{R_{t}}\sum_{r=1}^{R_{t}}\tau_{r,t}(a,b)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\tau}_{t}(a,b)=\frac{1}{R_{t}}\sum_{r=1}^{R_{t}}\tau_{r,t}(a,b),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of valid rounds for task <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. This produces a <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\times 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times 4</annotation></semantics></math> correlation matrix per task, summarizing grader consistency across domains such as mathematics, geoscience, and biology.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Application to SingleTask.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the SingleTask setting, each round produces rubric-based scores across nine dimensions <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{\text{Background/Creativity},\dots,\text{Impact/Logical~Coherence}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext>Background/Creativity</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext>Impact/Logical Coherence</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{\text{Background/Creativity},\dots,\text{Impact/Logical~Coherence}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each dimension <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d\in\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d\in\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, graders generate rankings of the players, and we compute <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{r,d}(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{r,d}(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math> for all pairs <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(a,b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(a,b)</annotation></semantics></math>. Averaging across rounds yields</p>
<table id="A1.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\tau}_{d}(a,b)=\frac{1}{R_{d}}\sum_{r=1}^{R_{d}}\tau_{r,d}(a,b)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>d</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\tau}_{d}(a,b)=\frac{1}{R_{d}}\sum_{r=1}^{R_{d}}\tau_{r,d}(a,b),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of valid rounds for dimension <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. This results in a <math id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4\times 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4\times 4</annotation></semantics></math> correlation matrix for each rubric dimension, capturing grader consistency in evaluating creativity, scientific rigor, and logical coherence across sections of the proposal.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Summary.</h5>

<div id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In both MultiTask and SingleTask, Kendall’s Tau provides a principled measure of inter-grader consistency. The distinction lies in the evaluation unit: <em id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tasks</em> for MultiTask versus <em id="A1.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">rubric dimensions</em> for SingleTask.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Detailed Demonstration of <span id="A2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span></h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To provide a concrete illustration of how <span id="A2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span> (RT) operates in practice, we present two representative demonstrations: one in the <em id="A2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">MultiTask</em> regime (Figure <a href="#A2.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Summary. ‣ Appendix B Detailed Demonstration of Roundtable Policy ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) and one in the <em id="A2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">SingleTask</em> regime (Figure <a href="#A2.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Summary. ‣ Appendix B Detailed Demonstration of Roundtable Policy ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). These examples showcase how RT transforms fragmented outputs from multiple LLMs into coherent, factually grounded, and narratively rigorous responses.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">MultiTask demonstration.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A2.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Summary. ‣ Appendix B Detailed Demonstration of Roundtable Policy ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> presents a representative MultiTask round, where nine LLM <em id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">players</em> respond simultaneously to a heterogeneous set of scientific queries spanning <em id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mechanics, thermodynamics, semiconductor physics, mathematics, geoscience, and biology</em>. Individual player outputs illustrate the limitations of single-model reasoning: some provide only partial explanations (e.g., noting that paper clips corrode without addressing stress relaxation), others introduce inaccuracies (e.g., oversimplifying nucleation–growth dynamics), and several fail to maintain consistency across domains (e.g., contradictory accounts of polymer glass transition). These fragmented outputs reflect the heterogeneity of LLM competencies—stronger players excel in certain areas, while weaker ones often produce vague or under-specified responses.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">By contrast, the fused response generated by RT demonstrates marked improvements in both <em id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">factual coverage</em> and <em id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reasoning depth</em>. For instance, RT not only describes corrosion of paper clips but also contextualizes long-term degradation and mechanical implications; in the nucleation–growth problem, RT integrates complementary insights into thermodynamic driving forces and kinetic limitations, producing a textbook-quality explanation. Similarly, in the polymer glass transition question, RT combines molecular structure, chain length, and plasticizer effects into a unified account that no single player captured in full.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Committee grading further substantiates these gains: RT exhibits higher factual accuracy and reduced uncertainty intervals across tasks. Improvements are particularly pronounced in symbolic and reasoning-intensive domains such as thermodynamics and mathematics, where RT delivers correct solutions while clarifying intermediate reasoning steps. These results highlight how RT balances correctness and clarity across heterogeneous domains.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">SingleTask demonstration.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A2.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ Summary. ‣ Appendix B Detailed Demonstration of Roundtable Policy ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> illustrates the SingleTask regime, where each player generates a three-paragraph scientific proposal (<em id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Background, Methodology, Impact</em>). Individual drafts vary widely in creativity, methodological depth, and logical flow. Some emphasize novelty but lack technical detail; others offer rigorous methods but fail to contextualize the problem or articulate downstream impact. Such stylistic and structural inconsistencies exemplify the difficulty of producing a coherent <em id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">scientific narrative</em> with a single LLM.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, the fused proposal produced by RT exhibits a markedly stronger narrative arc. The <em id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Background</em> situates the research problem within its broader scientific context; the <em id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Methodology</em> clearly articulates technical contributions with precision; and the <em id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Impact</em> section highlights both scientific significance and societal relevance. Grading results confirm consistent improvements across all rubric dimensions—creativity, scientific rigor, and logical coherence—demonstrating that RT effectively mitigates individual stylistic biases while amplifying collective strengths. Even strong baseline players benefit: while their drafts are often polished in one dimension, RT integrates complementary insights to deliver uniformly high-quality writing.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Summary.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Together, these demonstrations show how <span id="A2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Roundtable Policy</span> transforms fragmented or uneven model outputs into coherent, high-quality responses. In the MultiTask regime, RT enhances cross-domain reasoning reliability by integrating complementary knowledge across scientific fields. In the SingleTask regime, RT substantially improves narrative structure and explanatory depth in long-form proposal writing. These examples reinforce the central claim that RT enables LLM ensembles to achieve more factual, rigorous, and narratively coherent outputs in scientific applications.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<figure id="A2.F6" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.16839v1/singletask_demo_fig.png" id="A2.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:548/870;" width="548" height="870" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span> <span id="A2.F6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Demonstration of the SingleTask.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<figure id="A2.F7" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.16839v1/multitask_demo_fig.png" id="A2.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:548/870;" width="548" height="870" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span> <span id="A2.F7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Demonstration of the MultiTask.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Benchmarking Datasets</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Dataset Annotation</h3>

<figure id="A3.F8" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatleft" style="width:50%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.16839v1/mse_dataset_fig.svg" id="A3.F8.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:274/192;" width="274" height="192"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 8: </span>
Distribution of the <span id="A3.F8.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MatSci – Bench</span> materials-science subset across six sub-domains. The inner ring denotes the number of expert-annotated Q/K pairs per domain, while the outer ring represents the total token count. The dataset is approximately balanced across categories such as <em id="A3.F8.10" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">macro mechanics</em>, <em id="A3.F8.11" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">micro mechanics</em>, <em id="A3.F8.12" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">optical &amp; microscopic</em>, <em id="A3.F8.13" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">semiconductor</em>, <em id="A3.F8.14" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">thermodynamical</em>, and <em id="A3.F8.15" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">electrical &amp; electronic</em>—supporting cross-scale and cross-modality reasoning.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce <span id="A3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">MatSci–Bench</span>, a heterogeneous benchmark designed to evaluate the scientific reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs) in materials science and beyond. The benchmark spans six carefully curated sub-domains: <em id="A3.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(i) Macro Mechanics</em>, <em id="A3.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(ii) Micro Mechanics</em>, <em id="A3.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(iii) Optical &amp; Microscopic</em>, <em id="A3.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(iv) Semiconductor Physics</em>, <em id="A3.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(v) Thermodynamical Systems</em>, and <em id="A3.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(vi) Electrical &amp; Electronic Properties</em>. As visualized in Figure <a href="#A3.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ C.1 Dataset Annotation ‣ Appendix C Benchmarking Datasets ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, these categories are balanced in both question count and token length, enabling systematic cross-domain evaluation of diverse reasoning skills.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This hierarchical structure compels models to integrate reasoning across physical length scales—from atomic-level diffusion and microstructure evolution to bulk elasticity and device performance—while combining symbolic, conceptual, and numerical reasoning. Each sub-domain is paired with task-specific rubrics assessing both <span id="A3.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(a)</span> factual accuracy and <span id="A3.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(b)</span> explanatory depth. To further capture reasoning quality, all model outputs must include a <em id="A3.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">chain-of-thought</em> rationale, which is evaluated through two complementary methods: (i) the automatic Causal Reasoning Index (<span id="A3.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CRI</span>), and (ii) a human-curated explanation rubric (details in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS4" title="C.4 MatSci–Bench ‣ Appendix C Benchmarking Datasets ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.4</span></a>). These fine-grained metrics allow for robust measurement of model performance and principled ablation studies. Table <a href="#A3.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Materials-science subset. ‣ C.1 Dataset Annotation ‣ Appendix C Benchmarking Datasets ‣ Roundtable Policy: Improving Scientific Reasoning and Narratives through Confidence-Weighted Consensus of LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> provides representative examples across four broader scientific areas—Biology, Geoscience, Mathematics, and Materials Science—highlighting domain-specific instruction design and diverse task objectives. Unlike prior benchmarks focused narrowly on short factual queries, our dataset emphasizes heterogeneous scientific tasks requiring numerical derivation, mechanistic explanation, and symbolic fluency.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Materials-science subset.</h5>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1274" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1274</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1274</annotation></semantics></math> materials-science questions are drawn from the 2006–2024 UCLA Materials Science and Engineering Ph.D. qualifying examinations. This provenance ensures originality, rigor, and relevance to authentic scientific reasoning. Each problem is annotated with: <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(i)</span> a numeric ground-truth answer rounded to three significant digits, <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(ii)</span> an exact <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_LaTeX_logo" style="letter-spacing:-0.2em; margin-right:0.1em;">L<span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.4ex;font-variant:small-caps;;">a</span>T<span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-0.2ex;font-variant:small-caps;font-size:120%;">e</span>X</span> expression preserving symbolic fidelity, and <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(iii)</span> a step-by-step expert-verified solution. All annotations are fully compliant with <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_LaTeX_logo" style="letter-spacing:-0.2em; margin-right:0.1em;">L<span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:0.4ex;font-variant:small-caps;;">a</span>T<span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5.2" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:-0.2ex;font-variant:small-caps;font-size:120%;">e</span>X</span> and <span id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Markdown</span> standards, ensuring reproducibility and integration with downstream evaluation pipelines.</p>
</div>
